DILG Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Center, EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, West Triangle, Quezon City http://wuw.dilg.com.ph 20 SEP 0!LG OPiNlON NO. ATTY. JORICO FAVOR BAYAUA Vice Mayor Conner, Apayao Dear Vice Mayor Bayaua: This has reference to your letter dated 26 June 2019 to this Department requesting for legal opinion and/or clarification on whether DILG Opinion Nos. 131 series of 2010 and 02 series of 2017 are still being enforced, and being a Vice Mayor and practicing lawyer by profession, if you are required to secure an Authority to Practice Profession. In reply to the first query, we answer in affirmative. The above- mentioned opinions are still effective. In your second query on whether you are required to secure an authority to practice your profession. Settled is the rule that a Vice Mayor is a member of the Sanggunian Bayan. Consequently, Section 90 of the LGC, which pertains to the practice of profession of a local elective official shall apply to you. The said provision provides: "Section 90. Practice of Profession. — (a) AI/ governors, city and municipal mayors are prohibited from practicing their profession or engaging in any occupation other that the exercise of their functions as local chief executives. (b) Sanggunian members may practice their professions, engage in any occupation, or teach in schools except during sessions hours: Provided, That sanggunian members who are also members of the Bar shall not: (1) (2) Appear as counsel before any court in any civil case wherein a local government unit or any office, agency, or instrumentality of the of the government is the adverse party; Appear as counsel in any criminal case wherein an officer or employee of the national or local government is accused of an offense committed in relation to his office;

(3) (4) Collect any fee for their appearance in administrative proceedings involving the local government unit of which he is an official; and Use property and personnel of the government except when the sanggunian member concerned is defending the interest of the government. Apropos, Section 7 (b) (2) of RA 6713, otherwise known as the "Code of Conduct and Ethical Standards for Public Officials and Employees", provides that the public officials and employees, such as the vice mayor, may practice their profession provided that such practice will not conflict with their official functions. Pertinent provision of the said law provides: "Section 7. Prohibited Acts and transactions. — xxx xxx xxx (b) Outside employment and other activities related thereto. - Public officials and employees during their incumbency shall not: xxx xxx xxx (2) Engage in the private practice of their profession unless authorized by the Constitution or law, provided, that such practice will not conflict or tend to conflict With their Official functions: or" xxx xxx xxx In DILG Legal Opinion No. 085-96 dated 29 October 1996, we rendered an opinion regarding a sanggunian member's authority to practice of profession: "The Code allows sanggunian members to practice their profession, engage in any occupation, or teach in schools outside of session hours. There is no need for permission from other person or office (The Local Government Code of 1991. "The Key to National Development", Pimentel, p. 204). In addition, the Supreme Court in Catu vs. Rellosal reiterated the rule that the members of the sanggunian do not need to secure prior permission or authorization from any other person or office for them to practice their profession. The relevant portion of which reads: "On the other hand, members of the sangguniang panla/awigan, sangguniang panlungsod or sangguniang bayan may practice their professions, engage in any occupation, or teach in schools except during session hours. In other words, they may practice their professions, engage in any other I A.c. No. 5738, 19 February 2008, 546 SCRA 209

occupation, or teach in schools outside their session hours. Unlike governors, city mayors and municipal mayors, members of the sangguniang panlalawigan, sanggunian panlungsod or sanggunian bayan are required to hold regular sessions only at least once a week. Since the law itself grants them the authority to practice their professions, engage in any occupation or teach in schools outside session hours, there is 00 !onqer anv need for them to secure prior permission other person pffice for_ any Of these purposes'.' Hence, you can continue to practice your profession without securing prior authorization from any person or office. We hope we have enlightened you on the matter. Very truly yours, BY THE AUTHORITY OF THE ECRETARY MARIVE C. SACENDONCILLO, CESO 111 Unde ecretary for Local Government CC: Atty. Odilon L. Pasaraba Director, Bureau Of Local Government Supervision this Department RD Marlo L. Iringan DILG-CAR Brgy. Center, Upper Session Road Baguio City LLLS/I
